# Volute — Debug and automation surface

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Volute carries a small debugging and automation surface, `window.__volute`, so
the hall can be driven and inspected from code without touching the keyboard or
waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
build installs the finished surface on `window.__volute` as soon as the game has
initialized. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it, so it
is present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a read or a pose of the game's state. A pose arranges the hall,
and the game's own systems run from there exactly as they do in play: a core
placed through the surface advances, merges, and recoils as one the inlet
emitted, and a core released through it flies, strikes, and seats through the
same insertion a played shot uses.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Volute carries a small debugging and automation surface so the hall can be driven
and inspected from code without touching the keyboard or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
build's `initialize` returns the finished surface beside the state it built, as
the pair `[state, debug]`. The engine returns that same value from
`engine.debug`, and it is reached that way alone: nothing is installed on the
page. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it, so it is
present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a reading or a pose of `VoluteState`, written in the shape of
`update`, because the engine holds the state by value and nothing holds a
writable one. A pose takes the current state, as `DeepReadonly<VoluteState>`, and
returns the next `VoluteState`; a reading takes the state the same way and
returns what it read. A caller drives a pose through the engine's `apply`, as
`engine.apply((s) => debug.startLevel(s, 1))`, and a reading against the
engine's current state, as `debug.snapshot(engine.state)`. A pose arranges the
hall, and the game's own systems run from there exactly as they do in play: a
core placed through the surface advances, merges, and recoils as one the inlet
emitted, and a core released through it flies, strikes, and seats through the
same insertion a played shot uses. Where the surface's implementation lives
under `src/` is yours to decide; `src/game.ts` declares and exports its type,
`VoluteDebugApi`.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls it.
{{else}}
Volute carries a small debugging and automation surface so the hall can be driven
and inspected from code without touching the keyboard or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
game instance's `initialize` builds the finished surface and returns it. The
engine hands that same object back from `engine.debug`, and it is reached that
way alone: nothing is installed on the page. Every scenario driven from code
reaches the game through it, so it is present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a method that acts on the running game. A pose takes only the
arguments its heading names, returns nothing, and arranges the live world through
the same systems play uses, leaving the outcome to the frames that follow: a core
placed through the surface advances, merges, and recoils as one the inlet
emitted, and a core released through it flies, strikes, and seats through the
same insertion a played shot uses. A reading takes no arguments and returns plain
data, read off the world at the instant of the call. A pose that opens a level
takes effect no later than the end of the next advanced frame, so a caller
advances one frame after `reset` or `startLevel` before it poses, presses a key,
or reads further. Where the surface's implementation lives under
`src/` is yours to decide; the type your instance declares for it, the `D` of its
`GameInstance<D>`, is the surface this file specifies.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls it.
{{/if}}

## A render-free core

{{#if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}
The surface rests on the simulation being steppable apart from drawing. Every
rate this specification states is per second and every duration is in seconds,
integrated against the delta time the engine hands each tick, so a step means the
same thing whatever the frame rate. A `ConstantClock` of `1000 / 60` milliseconds
makes one frame exactly one simulation step of `1 / 60` second, which is the step
a scenario advances the engine by.
{{else}}
The surface rests on the simulation being steppable apart from drawing. The hall
advances on a fixed tick of `TICK_HZ` (`60`) ticks per second, each worth
`TICK_DT` (`1 / 60`) seconds: every frame's delta time accumulates, whole ticks
are consumed, and the remainder waits for the next frame, so a step means the
same thing whatever the frame rate. Every rate this specification states is per
second and every duration is in seconds, and one tick applies exactly `TICK_DT`
of them.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}
The world advances from its ticks and its input alone, independent of the canvas
and of wall-clock time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing
from the rendering pipeline, and every advance, merge, strike, insertion,
extraction, and cell is decided from the world alone.
{{else}}
Game state advances from ticks and input alone, independent of the canvas, of
the frame loop that measured the delta time, and of wall-clock time. The
dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the renderer, and
every advance, merge, strike, insertion, extraction, and cell is decided from the
state alone.
{{/if}}

The game draws at random in three places: the charges of the cores a level opens
with, the charge of each emitted core, and the charges the injector loads and
queues. `specs/channel.md` states the set each draw is uniform over, and the
surface poses the outcome a scenario needs in place of the draw: `poseTrain` the
cores on the channel, `setLoaded` and `setQueued` the injector's charges, and
`setNextEmitted` the charge of the next core the inlet emits. How the game draws
is yours.

Audio belongs to the ticks. A pose changes the state alone and sounds nothing;
the cues a scenario hears come from the ticks run after it.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath
the game, and the surface carries no operation for any of them. That layer
delivers the actions the game registers from the key bindings this specification
fixes, it reports the pointer in field units, and it draws and toggles the debug
overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `step` below.
{{else}}
The {{engine.name}} engine owns the clock, the keyboard, the pointer, and the
overlay, and documents them in the `engine/` directory, so the surface carries no
operation for any of them. The engine advances the game frame by frame and a
clock that supplies its own deltas takes it off real time, so a scenario pairs a
`ConstantClock` of `1000 / 60` milliseconds with `engine.advance` and steps
exactly one simulation step per frame. The engine drives the actions the game
registers, reports the pointer in field units, and draws and toggles the debug
overlay.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Volute's, under Diagnostics below.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`VOLUTE_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and
the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, booleans, and plain
arrays. Every arc position and distance is in the field's logical units, every
angle is in degrees measured from `+x` and increasing toward `+y`, and every
duration is in seconds.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation's first parameter is the current state, `DeepReadonly<VoluteState>`.
A pose returns the next `VoluteState`, built from the one it was handed, which it
leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read. The headings below name the
parameters that follow the state.
{{else}}

A pose takes only the parameters its heading names and returns nothing; a reading
takes none and returns what it read. Each operation acts on the live world at the
moment of the call.
{{/if}}

No pose declines. Each applies at the call whatever the screen, and an argument
outside its range is clamped to the nearest legal value or normalized as its
heading states. No pose decides an outcome either: every insertion, extraction,
score, chain step, mark, cell, clear, and ending comes from the ticks run after
the pose, so a scenario reads what happened from the snapshot.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setAutoStep(enabled)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the
frame loop feeding the wall clock's delta time into the tick accumulator, so the
hall advances only when `step` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to running
itself, which is how a build starts and how it is played.

Drawing is unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows
the state the most recent tick left.

### `step(ticks)`

Runs `ticks` whole simulation ticks, immediately and in order, each the full tick
followed by a render. `ticks` defaults to `1`.

Stepping while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall
clock is already doing, so a scenario driven from code calls
`setAutoStep(false)` first.

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `reset(state)`
{{else}}
### `reset()`
{{/if}}

Restores every declared field of the game's state to its title-screen value: the
`title` screen, the score `0`, the level `1`, the cells at `CELLS` (`3`), an empty
channel, no projectile, the pressure `0`, the chain step `1`, no active
machinery, no interlude running, the injector holding neither a loaded nor a
queued core with its cooldown at `0`, the aim at `270` degrees, no posed
emission charge, and `simTime` at `0`.

`muted` is deliberately untouched: muting is a player preference the runtime
owns. Because a reset restores exactly the declared fields, anything else the
build keeps across ticks is derived from them.

The inlet's gate and the train's gate are untouched as well, for the reason
`setEmission` and `setFeed` give below.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Whether the simulation advances on its own each frame is untouched for the same
reason: `setAutoStep` belongs to the caller driving the game, not to the run
being played, so a `reset` inside a stepped scenario leaves the game off the wall
clock.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `snapshot(state)`

A pure reading of `state`, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It returns no state, because it poses nothing.

### `setScreen(state, name)`
{{else}}
### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the running game, returned as the plain object under Snapshot
shape below. It changes nothing.

### `setScreen(name)`
{{/if}}

Sets the screen to `name`, one of the seven the state carries, and changes
nothing else: no level is opened, no channel is seeded, no timer is started, and
no interlude is set. A name outside the seven is taken as `title`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setLevel(state, level)`
{{else}}
### `setLevel(level)`
{{/if}}

Sets the level in play to `level`, a whole number clamped to `1` through
`LEVEL_COUNT` (`5`), and changes nothing else: the screen, the channel, the
quota, the pressure, the chain step, the active machinery, the projectiles, and
the injector are all left as they are. The level's feed speed and its charge set
follow the new value at once.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setScore(state, n)`, `setCells(state, n)`
{{else}}
### `setScore(n)`, `setCells(n)`
{{/if}}

`setScore` sets the run's score to `n`, a whole number clamped to at least `0`.
`setCells` sets the cells remaining to `n`, a whole number clamped to `0` through
`CELLS` (`3`). Neither ends a run or opens one: `setCells(0)` leaves the screen
exactly as it stands, and the ending a spent last cell reaches comes from the
ticks run after the pose.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setChainStep(state, k)`
{{else}}
### `setChainStep(k)`
{{/if}}

Sets the chain step an extraction scores at to `k`, a whole number clamped to at
least `1`, and restarts the window that returns the step to `1`, so the posed
step holds for `CHAIN_RESET` (`2.0` s) of play from the call.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `startLevel(state, level)`
{{else}}
### `startLevel(level)`
{{/if}}

Opens `level`, a whole number clamped to `1` through `LEVEL_COUNT` (`5`), exactly
as the interlude before it opens it: the screen becomes `playing`, the channel
holds the cores a level opens with, the quota is what a level start leaves it,
the pressure becomes `0`,
the chain step becomes `1`, the active machinery clears, every projectile is
discarded, and the injector draws a fresh loaded and queued core. The score and
the cells stay as they are.

This is the one operation that arranges more than one thing, because opening a
level IS the thing it poses: the seeded cores, the level's quota, and the fresh
draws are the level opening itself, and a caller that wants some other hall
assembles it from the single-field poses above.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `poseTrain(state, cores)`
{{else}}
### `poseTrain(cores)`
{{/if}}

Replaces every core on the channel with the cores given. `cores` is a list of
`[s, charge, mark]` triples: `s` is an arc position, clamped to at most `5000`;
`charge` is one of the five charge ids; and `mark` is one of the four machinery
kinds or `null` for an unmarked core. The cores are placed at exactly the arc
positions given, and the train orders them by descending `s` whatever order the
list arrived in, two cores given the same arc position keeping the order the
list gave them.

Segments follow from the spacing rule and every recoil hold is cleared, so a
posed train advances on the tick after the call. The quota, the pressure, the
chain step, the active machinery, the projectiles, and the injector are left
as they are.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `clearTrain(state)`
{{else}}
### `clearTrain()`
{{/if}}

Removes every core from the channel and every projectile. Nothing extracts,
nothing scores, no cell is spent, and the level, the quota, the pressure, the
chain step, the active machinery, and the injector are left as they are.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setLoaded(state, charge)`, `setQueued(state, charge)`
{{else}}
### `setLoaded(charge)`, `setQueued(charge)`
{{/if}}

Sets the core the injector holds loaded, and the one it holds queued, to
`charge`, one of the five charge ids, and changes nothing else: no core is
released, and the aim and the cooldown are untouched.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setAim(state, angleDegrees)`
{{else}}
### `setAim(angleDegrees)`
{{/if}}

Sets the aim to `angleDegrees`, normalized into `[0, 360)`, and does nothing
else: no core is released, the cooldown is untouched, and the loaded and queued
cores stay as they are.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `fire(state)`
{{else}}
### `fire()`
{{/if}}

Releases the loaded core along the current aim through the same path the fire
control takes: the core leaves the injector as a projectile, the queued core
becomes loaded, a fresh core is drawn as queued, and the cooldown is set as a
played shot sets it. Any cooldown outstanding at the call is cleared first, so
the call always launches, and a call made while the injector holds no loaded core
draws one first.

The flight, the strike, the insertion, and any extraction the insertion causes
come from the ticks that follow.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPressure(state, value)`
{{else}}
### `setPressure(value)`
{{/if}}

Sets the pressure to `value`, clamped to `0` through `100`. The feed speed the
snapshot reports follows the new value at once, and the rise, the bleed, and the
drop per extracted core run from it on the next tick.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setQuotaRemaining(state, n)`
{{else}}
### `setQuotaRemaining(n)`
{{/if}}

Sets the cores the inlet has left to emit this level to `n`, a whole number
clamped to `0` through the level's quota. The count of cores emitted this level
is the level's quota less what remains, so which of the following emissions carry
a mark, and which kind each mark is, follow the new value.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setEmission(state, enabled)`
{{else}}
### `setEmission(enabled)`
{{/if}}

Holds the inlet, and lets it go again. `setEmission(false)` stops step 7 of the
tick order `specs/channel.md` fixes: no core is emitted, whatever the quota and
whatever the tail has cleared, and the quota is left where it stands.
`setEmission(true)` returns the inlet to emitting by its own rule. It is
independent of the quota, so a hall whose quota is untouched and whose inlet is
held emits nothing and is never cleared for an exhausted quota.

The inlet emits when a game opens, and a `reset` and a `startLevel` both leave
this where the caller put it, because it belongs to the caller driving the game
rather than to the run being played. A charge `setNextEmitted` posed waits
behind a held inlet and is taken by the first emission after it is let go.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setNextEmitted(state, charge)`
{{else}}
### `setNextEmitted(charge)`
{{/if}}

Poses the charge of the next core the inlet emits. `charge` is one of the five
charge ids, or `null` to clear a standing pose; any other value is taken as
`null`. The next emission places a core carrying `charge` in place of the draw
`specs/channel.md` states, whatever the channel holds, and that emission consumes
the pose, so every emission after it is drawn again until the next call. A call
made while a pose stands replaces it.

Nothing else moves: the emitted core's mark, the quota, the inlet's gate, and
the charges the injector holds are as they would otherwise be. The snapshot
reports the pose as `nextEmitted`, and `null` while none stands.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setFeed(state, enabled)`
{{else}}
### `setFeed(enabled)`
{{/if}}

Holds the train where it stands, and lets it advance again. `setFeed(false)`
stops step 2 of that same tick order: no segment advances, no merge follows from
an advance, and an active `backflow` moves nothing. `setFeed(true)` returns the
train to advancing by its own rules.

Every other step runs unchanged while the train is held: timers fall, projectiles
fly, a strike seats and the run it completes is extracted, a removal recoils what
is behind it, pressure rises and bleeds, a core standing at the intake spends a
cell, and the inlet emits. The train advances when a game opens, and a `reset`
and a `startLevel` both leave this where the caller put it, for the same reason
the inlet's gate is left alone.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `grantMachinery(state, kind)`
{{else}}
### `grantMachinery(kind)`
{{/if}}

Grants `kind`, one of the three timed machinery kinds — `choke`, `backflow`, or
`sightline` — exactly as extracting a run holding a mark of that kind grants it:
it becomes the active machinery at its full duration, replacing whatever was
active and restarting its timer. A `kind` naming none of the three is taken as
`choke`.

`bore` is not granted here. It removes cores and scores the moment it resolves,
and no pose decides an outcome, so a caller that wants a bore poses a run
carrying a `bore` mark and lets the ticks extract it.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `pause(state)`, `resume(state)`
{{else}}
### `pause()`, `resume()`
{{/if}}

`pause` poses the pause control, moving the screen to `paused`; `resume` poses it
again, returning the screen to `playing`. Neither touches anything else.

## Snapshot shape

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`snapshot` returns:
{{else}}
`snapshot()` returns:
{{/if}}

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "playing" | "paused" | "cleared"
        | "setback" | "gameover" | "victory",
  score: <number>,
  level: <number>,               // 1 through 5
  cells: <number>,
  quotaRemaining: <number>,      // cores the inlet has left to emit this level
  emitted: <number>,             // the level's quota less quotaRemaining
  pressure: <number>,            // 0 through 100
  feedSpeed: <number>,           // the effective feed speed, units/s
  chainStep: <number>,
  chainTimer: <number>,          // seconds left before the step returns to 1
  interlude: <number>,           // seconds left of the interlude, else 0
  danger: <boolean>,

  // Every core on the channel, head first.
  train: [
    { s: <number>, x: <number>, y: <number>,   // the point s gives on the channel
      charge: <string>, mark: <string> | null,
      segment: <number> },       // 0 is the lead segment, rising toward the tail
  ],

  // The segments, head first, entry 0 the lead segment.
  segments: [
    { count: <number>, hold: <number> },   // hold is the recoil hold's seconds left
  ],

  injector: {
    aim: <number>,               // degrees
    cooldown: <number>,          // seconds until the injector may fire again
    loaded: <string> | null,
    queued: <string> | null,
  },

  // Every projectile.
  projectiles: [
    { x: <number>, y: <number>, angle: <number>, charge: <string> },
  ],

  machinery: { kind: <string>, remaining: <number> } | null,

  emission: <boolean>,           // whether the inlet emits
  feed: <boolean>,               // whether the train advances
  nextEmitted: <string> | null,  // the charge posed for the next emission
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
  autoStep: <boolean>,           // whether the frame loop advances the simulation
{{/if}}
  muted: <boolean>,
  simTime: <number>,             // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
}
```

The shape is fixed, and every field is present whatever the screen. `charge` is a
charge id, `mark` and `machinery.kind` are machinery kinds, `machinery` is
`null` while no timed machinery is active, and `nextEmitted` is a charge id
while a pose stands and `null` otherwise. Every field is read straight off the
game's state, so what the snapshot reports is what the game holds. Seven are
derived rather than stored:

| Field | Derived from |
| --- | --- |
| `emitted` | The level's quota less `quotaRemaining` |
| `feedSpeed` | The level's feed speed under the current pressure and choke |
| `danger` | The head's arc position against the danger threshold |
| `train[].x`, `train[].y` | The core's arc position walked along the channel |
| `train[].segment` | The spacing between consecutive cores |
| `segments[].count` | How many cores the segment holds |

{{#if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}
Two of the snapshot's figures are live reads rather than kept copies: `muted` is
the engine's own mute bit, read from the audio bus at the call, and `simTime` is
the frame clock's accumulated simulated time, which runs whatever the screen and
carries across level transitions.
{{else}}
The game keeps two of these honest every tick:

- `muted` mirrors the runtime's mute bit. Refresh it from the runtime in every
  update, since the runtime owns muting and this is the game's readable copy of
  it.
- `simTime` accumulates the elapsed time of every update, whatever the screen.
{{/if}}

## Diagnostics

The debug overlay is a read-only window on the same ground truth the snapshot
reports, drawn over the running game and toggled by the backtick key
(`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`), hidden until it is asked for. Register at
least the screen, the level with the score, the cells, the quota remaining, the
pressure with the feed speed, the chain step, the count of cores on the channel
with the head's arc position, the segment count, the danger flag, the loaded and
queued charges with the aim and the cooldown, the count of projectiles, and
the active machinery with its seconds left. Keep each one short enough to read on
a line, and keep every source a pure read, so watching the overlay leaves the
game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, it is shown and hidden by the backtick key, and it reads the game
without changing it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Volute's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the read,
as `DeepReadonly<VoluteState>`, and reads off that argument. Drawing the panel,
toggling it, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Volute's part, through the two
diagnostic registries the engine documents: a value that outlives a level on the
instance's registry, and a value read off the open world on the world's. Each
source is a function of no arguments that reads the live game at the moment it
runs and returns a short value. Drawing the panel, toggling it, and keeping it
read-only are the engine's.
{{/if}}
